{
  // ============================================================
  //  WriteSpeare — Desktop TypeScript Configuration
  // ============================================================
  //  A single config serves both compilation targets of the
  //  desktop app:
  //
  //    - The Electron main process, under `src/main/`, compiled
  //      by `tsc` (via the `build:main` script in package.json)
  //      to `dist/main/` and executed by Electron under Node's
  //      ESM loader.
  //
  //    - The React renderer, under `src/renderer/`, type-checked
  //      by the same `tsc` run but bundled into `dist/renderer/`
  //      by Vite. The compiled output of the renderer that `tsc`
  //      writes is unused — Vite produces the actual runtime
  //      bundle — but running the type check across it is
  //      valuable, because a type error in a `.tsx` file should
  //      fail `npm run type-check` the same way a type error in
  //      a `.ts` file does.
  //
  //  The two targets have different runtime environments (Node
  //  vs. browser) but share the same TypeScript language rules
  //  and the same import convention — the `.js`-suffixed
  //  specifier that points at a `.ts` source. That shared
  //  convention is what makes a single config possible: both
  //  targets resolve imports the same way, and the only
  //  environment-specific difference is the set of global types
  //  each target needs (Node globals for the main process, DOM
  //  globals for the renderer), which is expressed by listing
  //  both environments in the `lib` array.
  //
  //  The config is deliberately kept in lockstep with the
  //  server's tsconfig where the two overlap. Any change to the
  //  strictness flags here should be weighed against the same
  //  change on the server; the two configs are the same shape on
  //  purpose so that a reader who has internalised one has
  //  internalised both.
  // ============================================================

  "compilerOptions": {
    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  File Layout
    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  `rootDir` is `src/` so that compiled paths mirror source
    //  paths: `src/main/index.ts` → `dist/main/index.js`. The
    //  `main` field of package.json points at
    //  `dist/main/index.js`, so this mapping is what makes the
    //  Electron entry point resolvable.
    //
    //  `outDir` is `dist/`, the same directory the renderer
    //  build writes to. Vite writes to `dist/renderer/` (per
    //  `build.outDir` in vite.config.ts); `tsc` writes to
    //  whatever subdirectory each source file's path dictates.
    //  The two writers never collide, because Vite clears only
    //  its own subdirectory and `tsc` never writes to
    //  `dist/renderer/`.
    "rootDir": "./src",
    "outDir": "./dist",

    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  Environment Settings
    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  `module` and `moduleResolution` are `NodeNext`, matching
    //  the server config and the codebase-wide convention of
    //  `.js`-suffixed import specifiers that resolve to `.ts`
    //  sources. NodeNext is the only resolution mode that
    //  requires the runtime extension to be present in the
    //  source, which is exactly what the codebase does — so
    //  NodeNext is the only mode that will accept the imports
    //  as written.
    //
    //  `target` is ES2022. Electron 44 ships a modern V8 that
    //  supports all of ES2022 natively, and the renderer is
    //  bundled by Vite which does not care about the target. The
    //  choice between ES2020 and ES2022 is invisible to the
    //  running app; ES2022 is used here for consistency with the
    //  `lib` list below.
    //
    //  `lib` lists both DOM and ES2022. DOM is needed by the
    //  renderer (for `document`, `window`, `HTMLElement`, and
    //  every other browser global). ES2022 is needed by both
    //  targets. Listing both libraries in a single config means
    //  a main-process file could technically reference a DOM
    //  global without the compiler complaining — an acceptably
    //  small risk given the alternative would be two configs and
    //  a project-references setup that the plan does not
    //  declare.
    //
    //  `jsx` is `react-jsx`, the automatic JSX runtime introduced
    //  in React 17 and used exclusively by React 19. It means
    //  `.tsx` files do not need to `import React from 'react'`
    //  at the top — the compiler inserts the runtime import
    //  automatically.
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "jsx": "react-jsx",

    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  Outputs
    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  Sourcemaps are emitted for both targets. Vite produces
    //  its own renderer sourcemaps (see `build.sourcemap` in
    //  vite.config.ts); the sourcemaps produced here cover the
    //  main-process build, which is what a stack trace from the
    //  Electron main process will need in order to be readable.
    "sourceMap": true,

    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  Strict Typechecking
    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  Identical set to the server config. See the server's
    //  tsconfig.json for the reasoning behind each flag; the
    //  same reasoning applies here verbatim.
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,

    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  Style / Hygiene
    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature": true,

    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  Recommended / Module Hygiene
    // ----------------------------------------------------------
    //  `isolatedModules` is required by Vite. Vite's transform
    //  pipeline handles one file at a time and cannot see the
    //  whole module graph, so it depends on the compiler having
    //  already enforced that every file is independently
    //  transpilable. This flag is what guarantees that.
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",

    //  `skipLibCheck` is enabled — even more important for the
    //  desktop app than for the server, because Electron's
    //  bundled `.d.ts` files are large and occasionally reference
    //  types from other libraries that are not installed in a
    //  given configuration. Skipping the check means a `.d.ts`
    //  file authored by a third party cannot break the app's
    //  build by being slightly out of date with its own
    //  dependencies.
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    //  `esModuleInterop` and `allowSyntheticDefaultImports` are
    //  both enabled, matching the server config. They let code
    //  written against CommonJS modules (some Electron packages,
    //  some Node builtins) be imported with a default-import
    //  syntax that would otherwise be a type error.
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true,

    //  `forceConsistentCasingInFileNames` is required on a
    //  project that builds on Windows and is likely to be
    //  checked out on a case-insensitive filesystem. It ensures
    //  that an import of `./Foo` does not accidentally resolve
    //  to `./foo` on the developer's machine and then fail on
    //  a case-sensitive CI runner.
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,

    //  `resolveJsonModule` is enabled so that a source file can
    //  import a `.json` file (for example, `package.json` for a
    //  version string, or a config fixture) with a typed
    //  import. No file currently does, but the flag costs
    //  nothing and prevents a future surprise.
    "resolveJsonModule": true
  },

  // ----------------------------------------------------------
  //  Scope
  // ----------------------------------------------------------
  //  Every source file under `src/` is included. The main
  //  process, the renderer, the features, the shared utilities —
  //  all of it — is type-checked by a single `tsc` run.
  //
  //  `exclude` covers the three directories that never contain
  //  TypeScript source: `node_modules/` (dependencies),
  //  `dist/` (compiled output), and `release/` (electron-builder
  //  packaging output). None of them would contain a `.ts` or
  //  `.tsx` file, but listing them explicitly is the correct
  //  hygiene and prevents a stray generated file from being
  //  pulled into the compilation.
  "include": ["src/**/*"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "release"]
}